1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
They rush soldiers to the battlefield

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
and haul the supplies they rely on to wage war.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
They're massive, fast, agile, and innovative.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
The Osprey, the Expeditionary Fighting Vehicle,

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
the KC-135 Stratotanker, Roll-On Roll-Off Ships,

6
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
and Chinook helicopters.

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:33,000
Wherever they're deployed, they can make or break the fight.

8
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:37,000
Now, more military movers on Modern Marbles.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Whenever we consider the vehicles used in war,

10
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:59,000
We think of aircraft carriers, jet fighters, and tanks.

11
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:01,000
But these stars among war machines

12
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,740
could never battle effectively without the support

13
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,940
of a different breed of military mover,

14
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,960
the workhorses that can move mountains,

15
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:14,960
mountains of supplies, arms, and high-tech equipment

16
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:18,960
to battle zones anywhere in the world.

17
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:21,000
Amateurs talk tactics.

18
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:23,400
Professionals talk logistics.

19
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:26,700
Getting the right mix of men and materiel

20
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,340
to the right place at the right time

21
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:30,940
is the essential precondition for victory in battle.

22
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,640
Modern armies are transporting men and materials

23
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:39,640
further and faster than ever before.

24
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:42,460
In the case of American military operations,

25
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:45,260
these tasks are often twice as difficult as those

26
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:47,460
of other countries.

27
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:51,060
The America's founding fathers, in their infinite foresight,

28
00:01:51,061 --> 00:01:53,860
Foresight had the wisdom to put the United States

29
00:01:53,861 --> 00:01:56,660
on the other side of the planet from everybody else.

30
00:01:56,661 --> 00:02:00,100
So logistics is important for American military operations

31
00:02:00,101 --> 00:02:02,000
in a way that it is for no other country.

32
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,520
American forces have to travel thousands of miles

33
00:02:08,521 --> 00:02:10,240
even to get close to the battlefront.

34
00:02:10,241 --> 00:02:12,620
And with America's forces engaged in a global war

35
00:02:12,621 --> 00:02:16,120
on terrorism on nearly every continent on the planet,

36
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:18,260
logistics is the lifeblood of that struggle.

37
00:02:18,261 --> 00:02:23,260
The job calls for planning, foresight, and daring.

38
00:02:23,261 --> 00:02:28,260
It also calls for technological ingenuity, such as that found

39
00:02:28,261 --> 00:02:31,260
on the new V-22 Osprey aircraft.

40
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:39,260
The Osprey is designed to combine the best qualities of a

41
00:02:39,261 --> 00:02:42,260
helicopter with the best qualities of a fixed-wing

42
00:02:42,261 --> 00:02:43,260
aircraft.

43
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:51,760
Military planners began recognizing the need for such

44
00:02:51,761 --> 00:02:55,500
an aircraft in 1980, after America's dramatic failure

45
00:02:55,501 --> 00:02:57,500
to rescue its hostages from Iran.

46
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,500
That secret mission called for two C-141 airplanes to fly the

47
00:03:06,501 --> 00:03:10,500
hostages out of Iran after they were rescued by commandos

48
00:03:10,501 --> 00:03:14,260
aboard eight Sikorsky RH-53D helicopters.

49
00:03:14,261 --> 00:03:19,260
to the helicopters. Prior to the attempt,

50
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:21,760
the helicopters were to rendezvous at a remote site

51
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:24,400
in the Iranian desert. Basically, they were

52
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:27,000
trying to execute a very complex mission with a combination

53
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:30,500
of fixed-wing and rotary-wing assets, fixed-wing airplanes

54
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,920
for the range, and helicopters to be able to go in and land

55
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:35,620
vertically and take off vertically.

56
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:38,520
Ultimately, that mission failed as a function of its complexity.

57
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:43,720
The mission self-destructed when sandstorms

58
00:03:43,721 --> 00:03:46,360
problems prevented some of the choppers from reaching the

59
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:48,300
rendezvous site.

60
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:51,900
This was compounded when another chopper, while taking off at the

61
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:55,540
desert staging area, crashed into a C-130 that was there to

62
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,900
supply the troops.

63
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,840
Eight servicemen were killed.

64
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:04,000
Coordinating both fixed and rotary-winged aircraft for a

65
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:09,880
complex 35-hour covert op had proved logistically disastrous.

66
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:12,920
The solution seemed to be combining the functions of both

67
00:04:12,921 --> 00:04:16,760
of both into one aircraft. Had they had a tilt rotor,

68
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:20,160
a V-22, they could have done the same mission in nine hours,

69
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:23,160
in one period of darkness, with a much greater probability

70
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:28,260
of success. In 1983,

71
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:31,500
the Bell and Boeing companies won contracts to design and build

72
00:04:31,580 --> 00:04:35,680
the aircraft that would become the Osprey.

73
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:40,820
Today, after 25 years, it's still in development.

74
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:43,060
Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico

75
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:46,060
is among the places where pilots are learning how to fly it.

76
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,560
We are building the operational cadre

77
00:04:49,660 --> 00:04:52,800
to go out and employ this aircraft for Special Operations

78
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:53,600
Command.

79
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:58,900
At the heart of the Osprey's technology

80
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,940
is its ability to transform itself from one type of aircraft

81
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:02,740
to another.

82
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:08,920
At the pilot's command, hydraulically controlled

83
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:11,920
motors in the aircraft's wingtips rotate its engines.

84
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,920
When they're in the rotor-up position, the aircraft is in its

85
00:05:20,921 --> 00:05:25,920
helicopter mode. When down, it's in its airplane

86
00:05:25,921 --> 00:05:31,920
mode. The Osprey eliminates the need

87
00:05:31,921 --> 00:05:36,920
for the long runways that fixed winged planes need to take off

88
00:05:36,921 --> 00:05:40,120
and greatly advances the speed and range of a traditional

89
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:41,560
helicopter.

90
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:44,220
Well, a helicopter flies 110 knots.

91
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:47,860
Around here, we plan our crew speed at 240 knots ground.

92
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:52,860
The helicopter range is somewhere in the area of in the 100,

93
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:56,440
but our combat radius is over 500 miles,

94
00:05:56,540 --> 00:05:58,280
and that's without air refueling.

95
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:04,340
The Osprey is powered by twin Rolls-Royce engines,

96
00:06:04,341 --> 00:06:08,240
each of which can generate 6,150 horsepower.

97
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:14,180
Flying the aircraft is a unique experience for its pilots.

98
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:18,660
It's not a difficult plane to fly.

99
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:21,220
It's actually very natural.

100
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,460
The one thing you need to get the hang of is working

101
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:26,100
with the fourth flight control, which is the nacelle movement

102
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:28,960
on the ends of the wings.

103
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:32,700
When you're flying at the high speed, en route airplane mode,

104
00:06:32,701 --> 00:06:35,200
airplane mode, it flies just like an airplane.

105
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:38,500
And the folks who have flown it who have an airplane background

106
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:39,500
comment on that.

107
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:48,040
At the terminal area, when you're coming in for the approach

108
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:49,960
and you've got the nacelles rotated up,

109
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:51,660
it flies very much like a helicopter.

110
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:59,560
The Osprey can carry 24 combat troops,

111
00:06:59,561 --> 00:07:03,000
or up to 20,000 pounds of internal cargo,

112
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:06,560
or 15,000 pounds of external cargo.

113
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:09,100
Yet for all its ingenuity and promise,

114
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:11,300
the Osprey has had a difficult development period.

115
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:18,320
Remarkably, this 1991 accident at Wilmington, Delaware

116
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:19,920
involved no fatal injuries.

117
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:27,320
But during three other training and testing

118
00:07:27,321 --> 00:07:29,120
of the Osprey.

119
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:32,020
30 men have been killed.

120
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:34,420
Critics of the Osprey claimed that one of the accidents

121
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:37,060
was the result of flaws in the aircraft's aerodynamics.

122
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,060
They claimed it was more susceptible than other

123
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:45,340
helicopters to a dangerous phenomenon called vortex ring

124
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:46,440
state.

125
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:53,940
Under normal conditions, the rotor blades of the Osprey

126
00:07:53,941 --> 00:07:57,580
and all helicopters direct airflow downwards, creating

127
00:07:57,581 --> 00:07:59,880
lift.

128
00:07:59,881 --> 00:08:03,540
Vortex ring state can occur when a helicopter descends rapidly

129
00:08:03,541 --> 00:08:08,080
without generating adequate forward airspeed.

130
00:08:08,081 --> 00:08:10,880
The rotor blades can start reprocessing the same air

131
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:14,000
continuously, causing the helicopter to suddenly lose

132
00:08:14,001 --> 00:08:16,000
lift.

133
00:08:16,001 --> 00:08:18,300
This could potentially trigger a crash.

134
00:08:18,301 --> 00:08:23,300
crash.

135
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:25,700
After conducting a series of tests,

136
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:28,340
the Defense Department stated that the Osprey is actually

137
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:31,780
less vulnerable to Vortex ring state, not more,

138
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:34,320
than other helicopters.

139
00:08:34,420 --> 00:08:36,380
They assert that the Osprey's other crashes

140
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:39,420
were the result of faulty engine compartment design

141
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:41,720
and software problems which have since been resolved.

142
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:46,700
I think that the Osprey has been

143
00:08:46,701 --> 00:08:50,700
to all of the normal ailments of any complex aircraft

144
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,700
and development.

145
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:53,700
They've had a lot of design deficiencies

146
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:56,200
that they've had to identify and fix.

147
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:58,040
It's had reliability problems.

148
00:08:58,140 --> 00:08:59,440
It's had maintenance problems.

149
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:01,240
In other words, it's a normal weapon system

150
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:04,160
undergoing the sort of developmental efforts

151
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:06,660
that is common to just about every other vehicle we've

152
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:09,060
seen.

153
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:11,360
When the Osprey is finally deployed,

154
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:13,260
it will join thousands of other helicopters

155
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:14,360
that have served in war.

156
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:20,300
Almost from their inception, helicopters have been targeted

157
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:21,300
for military duty.

158
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:28,240
A helicopter is a very attractive military transport

159
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:30,540
because it can take off vertically.

160
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:32,240
You don't have to build a big runway.

161
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:33,860
And it can land vertically.

162
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:37,060
You can insert troops and supply very close to the front line.

163
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:42,300
Helicopters were still in their early stages of development

164
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:45,200
at the beginning of World War II.

165
00:09:45,201 --> 00:09:49,380
However, the Germans did use them for reconnaissance missions,

166
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:53,380
and the Americans flew their Sikorsky R-4 to rescue downed

167
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:54,740
flyers in Burma.

168
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:02,580
During the Korean War, choppers transported wounded soldiers

169
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:04,720
to mash units and delivered cargo.

170
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:14,200
By the Vietnam War, improved models like the Bell 204 Huey

171
00:10:14,201 --> 00:10:19,700
proved invaluable for both combat and cargo transport.

172
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:23,040
Production facilities produced over 12,000 Hueys

173
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:28,140
between 1959 and 1976, more than any other helicopter

174
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:30,960
before or since.

175
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:33,480
The Huey greatly advanced helicopter design

176
00:10:33,580 --> 00:10:37,860
by being one of the first to use turboshaft engines.

177
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:40,100
Well, turboshaft engines were the things that really

178
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:43,020
turned helicopters into a practical proposition

179
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:44,480
proposition for the military.

180
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:46,920
Piston engines were, of course, used initially.

181
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:47,920
They were large.

182
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:48,820
They rattled.

183
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:49,960
They were high maintenance.

184
00:10:50,060 --> 00:10:52,020
And they had relatively low power density.

185
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:55,260
When you bring in turboshaft engines, a jet engine connected

186
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:58,000
to a shaft to power the rotor, you basically

187
00:10:58,100 --> 00:11:02,140
get something that has a much higher power density.

188
00:11:02,240 --> 00:11:05,280
A turboshaft engine has a spinning fan that sucks air

189
00:11:05,380 --> 00:11:08,840
into a combustion chamber, where it is mixed with fuel

190
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:10,880
and ignited.

191
00:11:10,881 --> 00:11:15,020
As the fuel burns, it produces hot gases, which shoot into the

192
00:11:15,021 --> 00:11:17,720
turbine, causing it to spin.

193
00:11:17,721 --> 00:11:21,120
A shaft connected to the turbine also spins, which turns the

194
00:11:21,121 --> 00:11:22,420
rotors branching off from it.

195
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:29,040
The turboshaft engine enabled designers to develop brawny

196
00:11:29,041 --> 00:11:35,380
military helicopters, like the Boeing CH-47 Chinook.

197
00:11:35,381 --> 00:11:39,780
It's been an aviation superstar since 1962.

198
00:11:39,781 --> 00:11:42,140
We're the primary assault platform in Afghanistan,

199
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:44,360
the primary heavy lift platform everywhere.

200
00:11:45,260 --> 00:11:47,660
In Iraq, we did a lot of cargo hauling,

201
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:50,240
internal loads with 463L Air Force pallets.

202
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:54,060
Here in the States and also overseas,

203
00:11:54,140 --> 00:11:56,000
we do a whole lot of sling load operations,

204
00:11:56,120 --> 00:11:57,480
carrying up to 18,000 pounds,

205
00:11:57,640 --> 00:11:58,800
really anywhere you need it to go,

206
00:11:58,980 --> 00:12:00,180
as long as you keep giving us gas.

207
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:05,360
The Chinook owes its enormous strength

208
00:12:05,361 --> 00:12:07,660
to not only its powerful turboshaft engines,

209
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:09,520
but also its rotor blades.

210
00:12:09,780 --> 00:12:14,780
It has two rotating rotor discs, each with three blades

211
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:16,280
for a total of six.

212
00:12:16,380 --> 00:12:18,420
The blades are oversized, some of the largest ones

213
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:19,920
in the Army inventory.

214
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:21,520
And the blades are required to be that big

215
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:23,420
so they can actually produce the lift that's

216
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,540
required to move the 18,000 pounds worth of cargo and payload.

217
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,940
The blades spin in opposite directions.

218
00:12:33,040 --> 00:12:36,140
What that does is the forward rotor spins counterclockwise

219
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:38,480
and the aft rotor spins clockwise.

220
00:12:38,481 --> 00:12:40,980
So what those do is those forces counteract each other.

221
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:42,520
And you don't need a tail rotor like you

222
00:12:42,620 --> 00:12:44,120
do on other helicopters.

223
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:45,720
The twisting or torque action that's

224
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:48,720
put on a helicopter is in an equal and opposite direction

225
00:12:48,820 --> 00:12:49,920
on the aft as the forward.

226
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:52,920
So that keeps the helicopter from needing the vertical rotor.

227
00:12:56,540 --> 00:13:01,540
The Chinook's exterior frame has changed little since 1962.

228
00:13:01,640 --> 00:13:03,440
But the powerhouse under the hood

229
00:13:03,540 --> 00:13:05,640
has been repeatedly revamped.

230
00:13:05,641 --> 00:13:08,640
Chinook is a one-of-a-type airframe,

231
00:13:08,740 --> 00:13:12,280
and the fact that the Boeing people thought ahead

232
00:13:12,380 --> 00:13:14,780
when they designed this bird has allowed it

233
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:18,380
to be upgraded several times with more powerful engines,

234
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:22,400
more powerful transmissions, and allowed the bird to modernize

235
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:23,900
as the Army has modernized.

236
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,800
The Chinook has a top speed of 170 knots

237
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:32,400
and can stay airborne about 2 and 1 half hours

238
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:35,400
without refueling.

239
00:13:35,401 --> 00:13:39,000
The number of soldiers it can haul varies with each assignment.

240
00:13:39,001 --> 00:13:43,000
CH-47 has 33 seats. It can carry 33 soldiers in the

241
00:13:43,001 --> 00:13:45,480
seats. Sometimes we do operations

242
00:13:45,481 --> 00:13:47,880
without the seats. We raise the seats up,

243
00:13:47,881 --> 00:13:51,640
and it's only standing room. I've seen as many as 100 soldiers

244
00:13:51,641 --> 00:13:57,160
get on at one time. The Chinook is not only adept at

245
00:13:57,161 --> 00:14:01,400
giving military personnel a lift, but also at lifting the supplies on

246
00:14:01,401 --> 00:14:04,200
which they rely. CH-47

247
00:14:04,201 --> 00:14:06,200
We usually train with a cement block.

248
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:09,880
It can range anywhere from 6,000 pounds to 18,000 pounds.

249
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:12,200
One that's currently being used is 10,000 pounds.

250
00:14:16,520 --> 00:14:20,020
As the Chinook continues to loom large in the skies,

251
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:23,920
another military mover is strutting its stuff on the land

252
00:14:24,020 --> 00:14:27,260
and water.

253
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:31,700
Press a button and watch it change from a road warrior

254
00:14:31,701 --> 00:14:36,200
a warship.

255
00:14:36,201 --> 00:14:38,900
During peacekeeping operations in Bosnia,

256
00:14:38,901 --> 00:14:45,260
16 Chinooks flew 2,222 hours, carried more than 3,000 passengers,

257
00:14:45,261 --> 00:14:48,160
and transported over 3 million pounds of cargo

258
00:14:48,161 --> 00:14:50,520
in just six months.

259
00:14:50,521 --> 00:14:53,560
More military movers will return on Modern Marvelous.

260
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:06,700
Storming a beach in combat is one of the most difficult

