00:17:19,320 --> 00:17:25,220
is a German-engineered MTU883 diesel engine.

303
00:17:25,320 --> 00:17:28,020
It's a 12-cylinder, 90-degree V design.

304
00:17:28,021 --> 00:17:32,660
creates 2,700 horsepower in water mode and 850 horsepower

305
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:33,460
in land mode.

306
00:17:36,460 --> 00:17:38,360
Drivers can channel the engine's horsepower

307
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:41,060
effectively using controls that serve double duty

308
00:17:41,160 --> 00:17:43,860
on land and sea.

309
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:45,980
This is the shift selector for the driver's station.

310
00:17:46,080 --> 00:17:48,180
And what this does is it enables the driver to pick

311
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:52,780
his different gear so that he can move around in land or water

312
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:53,580
mode.

313
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:56,780
And over here, we have the inertial navigation unit.

314
00:17:56,781 --> 00:18:00,920
Now, what this does is it enables the vehicle to have GPS

315
00:18:01,020 --> 00:18:04,780
so that we can decide on where we're at.

316
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:07,160
Amphibious crafts like the expeditionary fighting

317
00:18:07,260 --> 00:18:12,960
vehicle can trace their origins to inventor, Donald Roebling.

318
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:15,840
He developed the first effective amphibious vehicle just prior

319
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:18,840
to World War II.

320
00:18:18,940 --> 00:18:21,480
Since it could move both on land and water,

321
00:18:21,580 --> 00:18:22,880
he dubbed it the alligator.

322
00:18:22,881 --> 00:18:25,880
the alligator.

323
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:28,920
Grubling originally designed the alligator to be used as a rescue

324
00:18:29,020 --> 00:18:32,920
vehicle in swamp areas, where it could pass from dry to wet

325
00:18:33,020 --> 00:18:35,620
areas quickly.

326
00:18:35,720 --> 00:18:38,020
But the Marines saw it as a machine that could help them

327
00:18:38,120 --> 00:18:43,740
