697
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:15,460
We can offload that way.

698
00:39:15,560 --> 00:39:17,520
That ramp comes up into the side of the ship.

699
00:39:17,521 --> 00:39:19,280
And just like on the stern ramp,

700
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:21,520
cargo will drive up and down the ramp.

701
00:39:24,160 --> 00:39:27,260
This row row, the USMS Watkins,

702
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:29,060
is as large as some aircraft carriers.

703
00:39:31,860 --> 00:39:36,980
Its cavernous interior, spanning 393,000 square feet,

704
00:39:37,080 --> 00:39:38,580
is capable of carrying the equipment

705
00:39:38,680 --> 00:39:40,580
for an entire US Army task force.

706
00:39:43,780 --> 00:39:47,520
58 tanks, 48 other tracked vehicles,

707
00:39:47,521 --> 00:39:51,960
as well as 900 trucks and other wheeled vehicles.

708
00:39:52,060 --> 00:39:54,520
To maximize cargo movement, the Watkins also

709
00:39:54,620 --> 00:39:56,660
has an intricate set of ramps between decks.

710
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:02,960
There are seven levels total, starting

711
00:40:03,060 --> 00:40:06,380
on what we call A deck, which is the weather deck, all the way

712
00:40:06,480 --> 00:40:09,080
down to G deck, which is the very bottom deck.

713
00:40:09,180 --> 00:40:13,320
Now, there are several ramps on the upper levels, from C, B,

714
00:40:13,420 --> 00:40:14,420
and A deck.

715
00:40:14,520 --> 00:40:17,520
The three top decks have quite a few varied ramps.

716
00:40:17,521 --> 00:40:22,220
All the ramps going up and down from sea deck down

717
00:40:22,320 --> 00:40:23,420
are on the port side.

718
00:40:23,520 --> 00:40:26,760
So everything kind of drives around in a clockwise position

719
00:40:26,860 --> 00:40:29,060
going down and down and down, and then obviously

720
00:40:29,160 --> 00:40:31,060
counterclockwise coming back up.

721
00:40:33,200 --> 00:40:35,380
Propelling the 950-foot Watkins

722
00:40:35,480 --> 00:40:37,880
and its nine sprawling acres of cargo

723
00:40:37,980 --> 00:40:43,880
requires some heavy-duty engines, heavy-duty jet engines.

724
00:40:43,980 --> 00:40:47,520
The engines are General Electric LM22.

725
00:40:47,521 --> 00:40:52,520
which are 32,000 shaft horsepower each, two of them.

726
00:40:52,860 --> 00:40:56,900
This is an aero derivative of the engine used

727
00:40:56,901 --> 00:41:00,520
in the 747 and 767 aircraft.

728
00:41:00,521 --> 00:41:03,580
The advantage of the gas turbine engine

729
00:41:03,581 --> 00:41:07,080
is high reliability, low maintenance,

730
00:41:07,081 --> 00:41:09,480
easy change out for overhauls.

731
00:41:11,700 --> 00:41:14,000
The engines can keep the Watkins thundering along

732
00:41:14,001 --> 00:41:16,220
at a top speed of 24 knots.

733
00:41:17,520 --> 00:41:20,820
As it moves, its cargo of vehicles stays put,

734
00:41:20,920 --> 00:41:23,160
thanks to a simple but effective technique.

735
00:41:23,260 --> 00:41:25,660
These are called vehicle lashing assemblies.

736
00:41:25,760 --> 00:41:29,060
It's what we use to tie down all of our equipment on board

737
00:41:29,160 --> 00:41:31,660
to prevent the equipment from getting underway

738
00:41:31,760 --> 00:41:33,160
while we're underway.

739
00:41:33,260 --> 00:41:35,180
Occasionally, when we're in heavy weather,

740
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:39,880
we can encounter heavy seas, and the ship can roll up to 20,

741
00:41:39,980 --> 00:41:41,580
maybe even 30 degrees.

742
00:41:41,680 --> 00:41:45,920
And these VLA's, they keep all of our cargo on board

743
00:41:46,020 --> 00:41:46,880
stationary.

744
00:41:46,881 --> 00:41:50,280
If one of them broke loose, it could start crashing into others,

745
00:41:50,380 --> 00:41:51,880
and then it would be a domino effect.

746
00:41:55,780 --> 00:41:59,920
Havoc could also result if the Watkins began taking on water.

747
00:42:00,020 --> 00:42:03,460
The solution is an elaborate system of watertight doors

748
00:42:03,560 --> 00:42:05,860
that crewmen can operate from the ship's deck

749
00:42:05,960 --> 00:42:07,640
and from its bridge.

750
00:42:07,740 --> 00:42:09,540
God forbid, say, we hit something,

751
00:42:09,640 --> 00:42:11,880
or one of the valves down below gives way

752
00:42:11,980 --> 00:42:14,180
and water starts coming into the ship.

753
00:42:14,181 --> 00:42:17,220
If you segregate the different parts of the ship with what we

754
00:42:17,320 --> 00:42:20,660
call watertight doors, that means if you hit a certain area

755
00:42:20,760 --> 00:42:23,160
of the ship, you only flood that part of the ship,

756
00:42:23,260 --> 00:42:24,460
and you don't sink the whole ship.

757
00:42:28,520 --> 00:42:31,300
Cargo ships have been a mainstay of military resupply

758
00:42:31,400 --> 00:42:33,740
for thousands of years.

759
00:42:33,840 --> 00:42:37,040
The basic principles haven't changed in centuries.

760
00:42:37,140 --> 00:42:40,580
Workers stored goods below deck and then unloaded them using

761
00:42:40,680 --> 00:42:42,500
cranes and hoists.

762
00:42:42,501 --> 00:42:45,540
But in the 20th century, the cargo shipping industry

763
00:42:45,640 --> 00:42:49,040
added an innovative twist to the process.

764
00:42:49,140 --> 00:42:52,340
The container ship was developed during the 1960s,

765
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:54,440
whereby the goods would be put in a container.

766
00:42:54,540 --> 00:42:56,740
The container would be put on board the ship,

767
00:42:56,840 --> 00:42:58,540
and then it would be just the container,

768
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:00,660
instead of the individual goods, would be unloaded

769
00:43:00,760 --> 00:43:03,660
in the foreign ports.

770
00:43:03,760 --> 00:43:05,660
The shipping industry developed roll-on,

771
00:43:05,760 --> 00:43:08,800
roll-off technology as it sought more efficient ways

772
00:43:08,900 --> 00:43:11,000
to load and unload cargo.

773
00:43:11,001 --> 00:43:13,000
you could drive.

774
00:43:13,100 --> 00:43:15,140
The initial application of Roro,

775
00:43:15,240 --> 00:43:18,480
roll on, roll off ships, was in transporting automobiles,

776
00:43:18,580 --> 00:43:21,040
in large measure transporting Japanese automobiles

777
00:43:21,140 --> 00:43:22,640
to the United States.

778
00:43:22,740 --> 00:43:25,120
In the late 1970s, when the United States decided

779
00:43:25,220 --> 00:43:29,080
that it needed to pre-position ships and military hardware

780
00:43:29,180 --> 00:43:32,120
close to the Persian Gulf to defend oil,

781
00:43:32,220 --> 00:43:35,420
Roro ships were a natural part of that initial squadron.

782
00:43:38,060 --> 00:43:40,200
At the end of an LMSR's voyage,

783
00:43:40,201 --> 00:43:42,660
vehicles can be driven right off the ship,

784
00:43:42,760 --> 00:43:45,100
ready for immediate deployment.

785
00:43:45,200 --> 00:43:48,600
Under ideal conditions, workers can unload a capacity cargo

786
00:43:48,700 --> 00:43:50,140
in just 48 hours.

787
00:43:54,640 --> 00:43:57,860
The roll-on, roll-off ships, like other military movers,

788
00:43:57,960 --> 00:44:01,060
are constantly on the go.

789
00:44:01,160 --> 00:44:04,560
These machines are much more than battle taxis.

790
00:44:04,660 --> 00:44:08,100
They're go-to equipment the military relies on to move

791
00:44:08,101 --> 00:44:12,740
to move troops and supplies to where they're needed most.

792
00:44:12,840 --> 00:44:15,900
They carry more, move faster, and they

793
00:44:16,000 --> 00:44:21,040
will continue to be the backbone of every military campaign.

